ure size 1600x500 with 3 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":39},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Based on the evaluation results across three transfer learning models trained on the Brain Cancer MRI dataset, InceptionV3 demonstrates the best overall performance.\n\nInceptionV3 achieved the highest accuracy of 98.75% with a ROC-AUC of 0.9936, correctly classifying 82 healthy and 76 tumor samples with only 2 misclassifications (False Negatives). Its sensitivity of 0.974 indicates strong ability to detect actual tumor cases, while its specificity of 1.000 means it produced zero false positives.\n\nDenseNet121 ranked second with an accuracy of 93.75% and the same ROC-AUC of 0.9936. It misclassified 10 tumor samples as healthy but maintained perfect specificity. ResNet50 performed the lowest among the three, achieving 90.62% accuracy with 15 false negatives, reflecting weaker sensitivity (0.808) in detecting tumor cases.\n\nIn a medical imaging context, sensitivity is a critical metric — a missed tumor (False Negative) carries higher clinical risk than a false alarm. InceptionV3's combination of high sensitivity (0.974), perfect specificity (1.000), and highest accuracy makes it the most suitable model for brain cancer detection in this study.","metadata":{}}]}